  "enhanced_information": "The patient's symptoms of pruritus, jaundice, and hepatosplenomegaly, combined with a history of ulcerative colitis, strongly suggest primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC). The standard diagnostic approach for PSC is imaging to identify bile duct strictures. Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) is the non-invasive gold standard for visualizing bile duct abnormalities and strictures associated with PSC, making it the most appropriate test to confirm the diagnosis. While endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) can also show bile duct strictures, it is more invasive and typically used for therapeutic interventions rather than initial diagnosis. Liver biopsy can show onion-skin fibrosis, but it is not the primary diagnostic tool for PSC. Ultrasound and contrast CT can detect bile duct abnormalities but are less specific than MRCP. Therefore, MRCP is the best choice to confirm the diagnosis of PSC in this patient.",
  "analysis": "The patient's symptoms of pruritus, jaundice, and hepatosplenomegaly, along with a history of ulcerative colitis, are highly suggestive of primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC). PSC is commonly associated with inflammatory bowel diseases like ulcerative colitis. The standard diagnostic approach for PSC involves imaging to identify characteristic bile duct strictures. Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) is the non-invasive gold standard for visualizing these bile duct abnormalities and strictures, making it the most appropriate test to confirm the diagnosis. While endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) can also show bile duct strictures, it is more invasive and typically reserved for therapeutic interventions rather than initial diagnosis. Liver biopsy can show specific histological features like onion-skin fibrosis, but it is not the primary diagnostic tool for PSC. Ultrasound and contrast CT can detect bile duct abnormalities but are less specific than MRCP. Therefore, MRCP is the best choice to confirm the diagnosis of PSC in this patient.",
